## 0. Context you must internalize first

AETHER is a no-code visual builder for algorithmic trading agents. The frontend (Next.js 16 / React 19 / TypeScript / Zustand / `@xyflow/react`) was built and frozen across Phases 0–9 as a fully mocked, `localStorage`-only client app. Backend work has since started:

- A FastAPI engine (`apps/engine/`) exists with: a node registry (`app/nodes/registry.py`), a DAG compiler (`app/graph/compiler.py`), a structural validator (`app/graph/validate.py`), and a backtest simulator (`app/engine/backtest_runner.py`) supporting `historical`, `paper`, `walk-forward`, and `monte-carlo` runs against real Binance OHLCV data cached in Postgres (`apps/engine/app/data/binance_ingest.py`).
- Supabase Postgres holds `profiles`, `bots`, `bot_versions`, `candles`, `backtest_runs`, `trades`, `equity_points` (see `supabase/migrations/*.sql`), all with RLS policies keyed on `auth.uid()`.
- The frontend has **already started** bridging to this real backend in two places:
  - `apps/web/lib/bots.ts` — real Supabase CRUD for bots (`listBots`, `getBot`, `createBot`, `updateBotGraph`, `updateBotMeta`, `deleteBot`, `saveBotVersion`), using `supabase.auth.getUser()`.
  - `apps/web/lib/engine.ts` — calls the FastAPI engine's `/backtest` and `/backtest/{run_id}` with a Supabase session's `access_token` as a Bearer token.
  - The Builder page (`apps/web/app/app/builder/[botId]/page.tsx`) already fetches the bot from Postgres via `getBot()`, autosaves the graph via `updateBotGraph()`, and saves version snapshots via `saveBotVersion()`. It falls back to the old mock `useWorkspace` store (`useBot(botId)`) only if the Postgres fetch fails.
  - The Backtest config and report pages already call `getBot()` and `getBacktest()` for real data, with the mock `useWorkspace` store used only as a fallback / local cache.

**This is a hybrid state, and it is fragile.** The rest of the app — `/app/bots` (bot list), the Bot Overview hub and its Settings tab, bot duplication, bot deletion, and JSON import — still read and write **exclusively** through the mock `useWorkspace` Zustand store (`apps/web/lib/workspace-store.ts`), which is pure `localStorage`, has nothing to do with Postgres, and will silently diverge from what the Builder and Backtest pages show. On top of that, it is **not yet confirmed** whether login/signup/logout in this codebase perform real Supabase Authentication or still just flip the old mock `useSession.authed` boolean from Phase 4 — if it's the latter, every Supabase call in `bots.ts`/`engine.ts` will fail with "User must be authenticated" the moment a real user without a fake session hits the app, because `supabase.auth.getUser()` will return `null`.

**Your job in this phase is to close both gaps**: make authentication real end-to-end, and make the entire bot lifecycle (list, view, create, rename, describe, tag, archive/delete, duplicate, import, version-restore) consistently backed by Postgres, with the local Zustand store demoted to a pure optimistic-UI cache that is always written to *after* a successful Supabase call — never treated as a source of truth for a real logged-in user.

Do **not** touch: presets (`myPresets`), the marketplace (`marketplacePresets`, `publishedPresets`), billing/credits, notifications/activity feeds, the live trading tab, or any node/agent execution logic. Those stay exactly as they are — still local mock data — until later phases. If a file you are editing also contains code for one of those out-of-scope areas, leave that code untouched and only modify the bot-lifecycle-related parts.

