## 2. Task 1 — Real Supabase Authentication (only if the audit shows it's missing/fake)

Implement using `@supabase/ssr` conventions (cookie-based session, refreshed on every request via middleware). If any of this is already correctly implemented per your audit, skip that sub-task and note why.

### 2.1 `apps/web/lib/supabase/client.ts` (browser client)
```ts
import { createBrowserClient } from '@supabase/ssr'

export function createClient() {
  return createBrowserClient(
    process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL!,
    process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY!,
  )
}
```

### 2.2 `apps/web/lib/supabase/server.ts` (server component / server action client)
Use `createServerClient` from `@supabase/ssr`, wired to `next/headers` cookies, following the standard Next.js App Router recipe (get/set/remove cookie handlers). This is required by `apps/web/lib/profile.ts`, which already imports `createClient` from here — confirm it matches this contract.

### 2.3 `apps/web/lib/supabase/middleware.ts` + `apps/web/middleware.ts`
Implement the standard `updateSession(request)` pattern: create a server client bound to the request/response cookies, call `supabase.auth.getUser()` to force a token refresh, and return the response with refreshed cookies attached. Wire `apps/web/middleware.ts` to call this on every request matching `/app/:path*` (and any other authenticated route), and **server-side redirect to `/login` if there is no user** — this is the real guard; the client-side check in `AppShell` becomes a secondary UX nicety, not the security boundary.

Matcher config should exclude static assets, `_next`, and public marketing/auth routes.

### 2.4 `apps/web/app/login/page.tsx`
Replace any mock auth flip with:
```ts
const supabase = createClient()
const { error } = await supabase.auth.signInWithPassword({ email, password })
if (error) { /* show inline error, do not redirect */ }
else { router.push('/app') } // or /onboarding/welcome — see 2.7
```
Keep the existing form/validation UI exactly as designed (Aether Glass primitives, inline error states) — only replace the submit handler's internals. OAuth buttons (Google/GitHub): if Supabase project OAuth providers aren't configured, wire them to `supabase.auth.signInWithOAuth({ provider: 'google' | 'github', options: { redirectTo: `${origin}/auth/callback` } })` but wrap in a try/catch that shows a graceful "OAuth not configured" toast rather than crashing, since we cannot assume provider credentials exist in every environment.

### 2.5 `apps/web/app/signup/page.tsx`
```ts
const { data, error } = await supabase.auth.signUp({
  email, password,
  options: { data: { full_name: name } },
